# Decoding the `mail(): Multiple or malformed newlines found in additional_header` Error

The error message `multiple or malformed newlines found in additional_header` is a classic indicator of header injection vulnerabilities, often triggered by how PHP's `mail()` function processes the `additional_headers` parameter. When this error appears suddenly on a server like Apache without any code changes, it usually points to a mismatch between your application logic and the specific security hardening applied to that server environment.

The core issue lies in how email headers are formatted. In RFC 2822, which governs internet email standards, header lines must be separated by a single carriage return-line feed sequence (`\r\n`). However, many modern mail servers, particularly those running on Linux distributions with strict security policies enabled (like the ones often found on shared hosting platforms such as 1&1), enforce this strictly. If your script concatenates headers without ensuring the line endings are exactly `\r\n`, or if user input inadvertently introduces extra newlines, the PHP SAPI layer throws an exception before the email is even sent.

This behavior often differs between environments. A script that works on a local development machine (typically running PHP via XAMPP, WAMP, or MAMP with loose `php.ini` settings) might fail on production if the server's `php.ini` has been updated to treat malformed headers as fatal errors rather than silently ignoring them or truncating them. The sudden appearance of this error suggests that either a system update changed the default behavior of the PHP mail extension, or a security patch was applied to the underlying MTA (Mail Transfer Agent) configuration.

To resolve this, you must audit how headers are constructed in your code. A common pattern involves iterating over an array of header names and values:

```php
$headers = [
    'From' => 'noreply@example.com',
    'Reply-To' => 'support@example.com',
];

// Incorrect approach that can trigger the error if values contain \n
foreach ($headers as $name => $value) {
    $headers[] = "$name: $value";
}

mail($to, $subject, $body, implode("\r\n", $headers));
```

The problem here is implicit: if `$value` contains a newline character (even unintentionally), or if the initial array definition isn't handled strictly, you risk creating multiple consecutive newlines. The fix requires explicit validation and formatting of every header line before concatenation. You should ensure that every header string ends with exactly one `\r\n`.

Additionally, be wary of user-supplied data in headers. If your application allows dynamic header names or values based on POST parameters, you must sanitize them rigorously. An attacker could inject a header like `X-Custom: value\r\nX-Injected: evil` which, if concatenated naively, creates multiple newlines and breaks the mail stream. Always escape or validate inputs to prevent header injection attacks.

If you are using Laravel, you might find that the built-in `Mail::send()` method abstracts these details away, but custom implementations require manual care. For high-security environments, consider switching from `php://mail` to SMTP via the `PHPMailer` library or a dedicated mail service API. These libraries handle line ending normalization internally and provide better error reporting, making them less susceptible to environment-specific quirks regarding header formatting.

Ultimately, this error is a safety mechanism preventing malformed emails from entering the system. While it can be annoying when debugging production issues, resolving it involves strict adherence to RFC standards in your header construction logic and ensuring your environment matches the expectations of the underlying mail transport agent.
